{"title":"Open Frequency","logline":"When a storm cuts off a small town, a ragtag crew of community radio volunteers uses a precarious hill relay to broadcast lifesaving updates, racing to keep shelters supplied and residents connected before the emergency worsens.","synopsis":"A powerful storm has isolated the town of Red Creek. With the bridge closed and cell towers down, the volunteers of the community radio station OPEN FREQUENCY, led by ARIA, NOAH, JUNE, and KELL, climb a water tower catwalk to rig a portable antenna to the hill relay. Their signal brings in a desperate radio call from the north shelter, which needs insulin and dry blankets, and the station immediately becomes the town's lifeline.

At the high school gym shelter, June coordinates updates on a whiteboard while Kell organizes supplies. A teenager named MAYA arrives asking the station to broadcast a search for her missing dog, COMET. June agrees, treating the small request as part of the community's needs, and the dog is later found safe. The night's work ends with the station restored and the crew still on air.

By morning, the studio has settled into a steady routine. Maya returns with a muddy but safe COMET, and June celebrates the reunion on air with a bulletin on road access, shelter supplies, and the recovered dog. The ON AIR sign holds steady as the crew returns to work, the broadcast having become a shared thread that holds the community together through the crisis.","act_structure":[{"act_number":1,"title":"Setup and Signal Recovery","start_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - NIGHT","end_scene":"EXT. WATER TOWER CATWALK - NIGHT","summary":"The station crew learns the bridge and cell towers are down and that the hill relay is their only chance to broadcast. Aria and Noah rig the antenna on the catwalk and pick up a distress call.","turning_points":["Kell arrives with the antenna and reports that the bridge is closed and cell towers are dead","The ON AIR sign fails, so the crew must restore the signal","On the catwalk, Noah plugs in the receiver and picks up a radio call asking for insulin and dry blankets at the north shelter","June answers Aria over the radio, confirming the relay works"]},{"act_number":2,"title":"Shelter Operations and the Missing Dog","start_scene":"INT. HIGH SCHOOL GYM SHELTER - PRE-DAWN","end_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - MORNING","summary":"At the gym shelter, June coordinates updates while Maya asks for help finding her dog. June agrees to broadcast the request, and the dog is recovered by morning.","turning_points":["Maya asks the station to announce her missing dog, COMET","June decides that community needs, including a lost dog, belong on the broadcast","Noah relays an update that the east road is open one lane and heavy trucks must wait","Maya returns with COMET, and the crew celebrates the recovery on air"]},{"act_number":3,"title":"Restored Routine","start_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - MORNING","end_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - MORNING","summary":"The studio stabilizes as the crew settles into a daily broadcast routine, with the ON AIR sign holding a solid glow and the team committed to serving the town.","turning_points":["June tapes the OPEN FREQUENCY sign over the door, marking the station's purpose","June broadcasts the morning bulletin on roads, shelters, and the recovered dog","June refuses to repeat the music too often, choosing to keep the broadcast fresh","The ON AIR sign steadies as the crew resumes work"]}],"themes":[{"theme":"Community as lifeline","description":"The station's value comes from connecting neighbors during a crisis, turning a small volunteer effort into a shared resource for the whole town.","evidence":["June: "If we're naming roads and insulin drops, we can name dogs."","Radio call: "north shelter needs insulin and dry blankets."","June's morning bulletin on road access, shelter stock, and the recovered dog"]},{"theme":"Human needs within crisis","description":"Large emergency logistics and small personal losses sit side by side, and the story treats both as worthy of attention.","evidence":["Maya: "He's family."","Kell sorts flashlight batteries while June records shelter updates","Maya returns with a muddy, sleepy COMET"]},{"theme":"Persistence against failure","description":"Equipment repeatedly fails, and the crew keeps fixing it, reflecting a hopeful refusal to give up on the broadcast.","evidence":["Aria: "Give me ten minutes and one miracle."","The ON AIR sign flickers, then dies again","The ON AIR sign steadies into a solid red glow"]}],"narrative_arc":"The story follows a classic three-act emergency structure with a light, warm tone. Act one establishes the crisis and the crew's effort to restore their signal, reaching a hopeful turn when a distress call is received. Act two widens the story to the shelter and the personal stakes of a missing dog, which resolves on a positive note. Act three returns to the studio with a restored routine, closing on a steady, reassuring image of the station back on air.","genre":"Drama","tone":"Warm, hopeful, and lightly comedic, with urgency from the storm","protagonist_journey":"Aria begins the story focused on technical problem-solving and a single miracle, and ends as part of a community-driven team. Her arc moves from individual effort to shared responsibility, shown when she and Noah bring the relay online and the station becomes a collective tool for the town.","central_conflict":"The volunteers must restore a broadcast link through a failing relay during a storm that has cut off the town, while meeting urgent shelter needs and handling smaller community requests.","setting_overview":"A small rural town called Red Creek during a violent storm and power outage. Key locations include a community radio studio running on emergency power, a water tower catwalk used for the hill relay, and a high school gym converted into an evacuation shelter. The bridge is closed, cell towers are down, and the town relies on radio for information.","confidence":0.85}
